# Backend Testing Guardrails

Apply these instructions whenever the user asks to plan, create, modify, review, debug,
or run tests for the Spring Boot backend, or when the user changes backend production
behavior that affects the backend coverage inventory.

## Required skill

Use the `spring-backend-flow-testing` skill for backend testing tasks.

Follow its two-phase process:

1. Analyze the scenario and present a test plan.
2. Wait for explicit user approval before implementation edits.

## Approval gate

Before creating, modifying, renaming, moving, or deleting backend test files, or before
changing backend production behavior under a separately approved production task:

* Present the exact proposed change.
* Identify the exact affected file paths.
* Identify the exact test cases or production behavior changes.
* Wait for explicit user approval.

Do not interpret the original testing request as approval to edit files.

Do not combine the planning phase and implementation phase into one step.

This Rule does not itself authorize production-code changes.

## Testing-only task restrictions

During a testing-only task:

* production files remain forbidden,
* `pom.xml` remains forbidden,
* main application configuration remains forbidden,
* only exact approved files under `src/test/**` may change,
* `docs/testing/backend-test-coverage-map.md` may change when required,
* approval does not authorize arbitrary changes across all `src/test/**` files,
* any additional file requires separate approval.

## Separately approved production-behavior tasks

For a separately approved backend production-behavior task:

* production edits require a separate explicit plan and approval,
* only exact production files named in that approved plan may change,
* only exact related test files named in that approved plan may change,
* `docs/testing/backend-test-coverage-map.md` must be updated in the same implementation slice,
* any additional production, test, configuration, or dependency file requires separate approval.

## Coverage map maintenance

Any approved backend testing or backend production-behavior change that affects the
backend verification inventory must update:

```text
docs/testing/backend-test-coverage-map.md
```

in the same implementation slice.

The plan must identify:

* the exact affected Coverage IDs,
* the exact affected map sections,
* whether coverage statuses change,
* whether metrics change,
* whether backlog or concerns change,
* or whether only verification metadata and the change log change.

Do not edit the coverage map during Plan Mode.

Use the exact status vocabulary:

`Complete for current contract`

Mocked-only coverage must not be marked `Complete for current contract` when relevant
HTTP, database, Spring Security filter-chain, or external-integration risks remain.

Update verification metadata using actual command results.

Recalculate metrics only when the numerator or denominator genuinely changes.

Update concerns or backlog only when supported by the current code and tests.

Add a `Coverage change log` entry for every approved slice.

List the exact affected Coverage IDs in the final report.

For pure test-quality refactors:

* update verification metadata,
* update the change log,
* do not increase coverage statuses or percentages unless genuinely new behavior is tested.

For production behavior changes without matching tests:

* update the production inventory,
* record the remaining testing gap,
* do not present the flow as fully verified or `Complete for current contract`.

If the map disagrees with the current code and tests, treat the current code and tests as
source of truth, update the map in the approved slice, and report the discrepancy.

## Allowed changes after approval

After explicit approval in a testing-only task, changes are permitted only under:

```text
src/test/**
docs/testing/backend-test-coverage-map.md
```

This includes:

```text
src/test/java/**
src/test/resources/**
```

Implement only the exact files and test cases included in the approved plan.

If additional changes become necessary, stop and request separate approval.

## Forbidden changes

During a backend testing task, never modify:

```text
src/main/**
pom.xml
mvnw
mvnw.cmd
application.properties
application.yml
docker-compose.yml
```

Also never:

* Modify production behavior as part of a testing-only task.
* Modify security or authorization logic during a testing-only task.
* Modify validation logic during a testing-only task.
* Modify exception handling during a testing-only task.
* Modify entities, repositories, services, or controllers during a testing-only task.
* Modify database constraints or migrations during a testing-only task.
* Add or update dependencies.
* Add Testcontainers or another testing framework without separate approval.
* Weaken assertions to make a test pass.
* Change an expected result merely to match an existing bug.
* Hide a failing test.
* Claim that a test passed without executing it.

## Production bugs

When a test exposes a probable production bug:

* Keep the test unchanged if it represents the approved requirement correctly.
* Do not fix the production code.
* Report the expected behavior.
* Report the actual behavior.
* Identify the likely production area involved.
* State that the production fix requires a separate task and explicit approval.

## Test execution

After approved changes:

* Run the focused test first when a meaningful focused suite exists.
* Run the relevant wider test suite when appropriate.
* Attempt the full test suite.
* Report the exact commands and results.
* Report blockers and existing unrelated failures honestly.

If the full suite fails or is blocked:

* record exactly what ran,
* record failures, errors, skipped tests, or environment blockers,
* do not mark affected Coverage IDs `Complete for current contract`,
* preserve the remaining gap in the coverage map,
* and do not fabricate passing results.

Running tests does not grant permission to modify files outside the approved scope.

## Final confirmation

A backend testing task or explicitly approved backend production-behavior task is not
complete until:

* focused tests were run when applicable,
* the full suite was attempted,
* actual results were reported honestly,
* affected coverage-map rows were updated,
* verification metadata was updated,
* a coverage change-log entry was added,
* and the final report listed the exact affected Coverage IDs.

Every implementation report must explicitly confirm:

* All changed files.
* Whether `docs/testing/backend-test-coverage-map.md` was changed.
* For a testing-only task, that no production file changed.
* For an explicitly approved production task, every changed production file and confirmation that no unapproved production file changed.
* Which commands were executed.
* Which tests passed, failed, or were skipped.
* Which Coverage IDs were updated.
* Which coverage-map sections were updated.
* Whether metrics changed or remained unchanged.
* Which remaining gaps are still open.
